#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186116445 :tutorial/content "**Complementary angles** are two angles whose measures have a sum of $90^\\circ,$ a right angle.\n\n![](images/27/2723049c087f5fa7b061bc9f1d4fb8c894c1e94aee3dfb994a237cd889a377e0.png)\n\nFor example, the angles in the diagram above are complementary because the sum of their measures is $90^\\circ\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n60^\\circ +30^\\circ = 90^\\circ\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "afcaca1a-2251-56a1-8a14-8094eb10881b" :tutorial/math-academy-id 13548 :tutorial/title "Introduction"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186521659 :tutorial/content "Now, consider the following diagram.\n\n![](images/02/02d8744bbc39ac9ed10d276071599f9008185023ebdfc3aaf97aebd498d82629.png)\n\nLet's solve for $x$ given the figure above.\n\nFrom the diagram, we have two complementary angles, so\n\n$$\n46^\\circ + (8x + 4^\\circ) = 90^\\circ.\n$$\n\nHence, we can solve for $x$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{46}^{∘}+(8x+{4}^{∘}) & ={90}^{∘} \\\\ 8x+{50}^{∘} & ={90}^{∘} \\\\ 8x & ={40}^{∘} \\\\ x & ={5}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "62b39d73-2c91-59ba-9e98-44a61a98c7b4" :tutorial/math-academy-id 15157 :tutorial/title "Working With Diagrams"}]}
